<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-shape" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Shape of Data </title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            At the end of this section you will able to:
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
          <li> <p> Accurately describe the shape of a graph as uniform, right skewed, left skewed, or bell shaped and symmetrical.  </p> </li>
          <li> <p> Correctly identify the approximate center of a distribution. </p> </li>
          <li> <p> Correctly estimate the range of values of a distribution. </p> </li>
        </ol>
      </objectives>
      

<p> When we display numerical data in a histogram we often look at its overall shape. If the data has similar frequencies, the bars are about the same size. This is knows as a <term> uniform </term> distribution. The center of the distribution is around the center of the middle intervals. </p>
<image source="ShapeUniform.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription> uniform with equal heights</shortdescription>
            </image>
<p>      </p>
<p> A distribution the is more bell-shaped, meaning most of the data is in the center with few on the tails and appears symmetric, is referred to as <term> normally </term> distributed. Symmetric refers to imagining folding the graph in half about the middle and the two sides will align.  If one of the tails is longer than the other, we refer to it as being skewed.</p>
<image source="ShapeSym.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription> symmetric with hump in middle</shortdescription>
            </image>
<p>      </p>

<p> If the data tends to cluster on the right side, and we see a few values on the left side, this is known as <term> left skewed </term> since the tail goes to the left of the data. This indicates the mean will be left of the median. </p>
<image source="ShapeLeft.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription> left skew</shortdescription>
            </image>
<p>      </p>

<p> If the data tends to cluster on the left side, and we see a few values on the right side, this is known as <term> right skewed </term> since the tail goes to the right of the data. This indicates the mean will be right of the median.</p>
<image source="ShapeRight.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription> right skew</shortdescription>
            </image>
<p>      </p>

<p> Sometimes the graph can have multiple bumps showing certain values occur more often than others. This can be an example of <term> bimodal </term> (meaning two values occur more often than others). </p>
<image source="ShapeBimodal.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription> bimodal with two humps</shortdescription>
            </image>


<p> <term> Practice Problems </term></p> 
<exercise> <title> Shape of Pets</title>
<statement> 
<p> Describe the shape of the distribution for the number of pets students have. </p>
<figure xml:id="PetDotplot2">
            <caption>Dotplot for Number of Pets</caption>
            <image source="PetDotplot.png" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>Students had between 0 and 8 pets, with most students having 0 pets, 11 students had none, 13 students had none, 11 students had1 pet, 9 students had 2 pets, 5 students had 3 pets, no students had 4 pets, 1 student had 5, 6, 7, and 8 pets each.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure> 
</statement>
<solution>
<p> It is right skewed since the tail goes to the right.</p>
</solution>
</exercise> 

<exercise> <title> Shape Credit Hours</title>
<statement> 
<p> Describe the shape of the distribution for the number of credit hours students are taking. </p>
<figure xml:id="CreditHistogram2">
            <caption>Histogram for number of credits</caption>
            <image source="CreditHisto.png" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>Students take between 12 and 21 credits with more taking 15-16 credit hours.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure> 

</statement>
<solution>
<p> It is roughly bell-shaped since most values are around 14-17 credits and fewer at the tails.</p>
</solution>
</exercise> 

<exercise> <title> Shape of Tattoos</title>
<statement> 
<p> Describe the shape of the distribution for the number of tattoos students have. </p>
<figure xml:id="TattooDotplot2">
            <caption>Dotplot of Tattoos</caption>
            <image source="DotplotTattoos.png" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>Students have between 0 and 12 tattoos with 0 occurring more often than more tattoos.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure> 
</statement>
<solution>
<p> It is right skewed since the tail goes to the right. Most students have no tattoos.</p>
</solution>
</exercise> 
</section>